Z||000002||MinnGreenGT||08-20-2001||02:32 PM||||I have thought about just gluing wood veneer to the origional faces... gives you REAL wood and the stability of the original plastic.  AND there are a lot of unique woods that can be stained and varnished to suit.  Check with a local woodcraft shop for supplies and pricing!  Roy- I am interested to see what you come up with!<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngtsig3.jpg">     <P>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||08-20-2001||06:37 PM||||If I ever get away from my current job, I plan on doing the same as Roy but with cherry.  The panels will be thicker than a veneer, but I think they'll still look good if the edges are beveled just right.  I'm planning on doing all including the shifter surround (I plan on eliminating the ashtrays--that's what windows are for <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ).  All it should take is a good straight edge, a jig saw and a router (and maybe a Dremel to do some finish sanding).<P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000004||Roy Grooms||08-21-2001||07:41 PM|||| Well I picked out red oak veneer, and I'm useing the plactic plate as a template , had to trace it out on paper and transfer it to the wood,just got done cutting it out and did the speedo hole. if you are not use to cutting wood don't try this it's hard and time comsumeing.I'm going to do the radio compartment and the spot to the right with the insert. up dates later!<p>[This message has been edited by Roy Grooms (edited 08-21-2001).]||||reg||1
